# Every conversion the filings make, as an edge between two units. One row per fusion part that
# carries a factor: the unit it converts from, the unit it converts to, and the three points.
#
# The ends are filed facts
#   Nothing here parses a unit string. A factor's own unit reads `GPU-hour per GPU`, and only the
#   denominator half of that is structural, so reading the pair off the label would mean splitting
#   free text on a preposition, a `LIKE '% per %'` that has no place here. It is not needed: a
#   factor converts a part layer into its composed layer, and both of those layers file their own
#   unit. The edge is the two layers, and the label is only a label.
#
# A crossing with no factor at all
#   An edge can cross a unit boundary with no factor at all, and finding those is what this
#   relation is for. `pessoas` to `people`, and `turnos por semana` to `shifts per week` (twice),
#   are three such crossings among the loaded documents. A composition like that asserts that a
#   Portuguese unit and an English one are the same thing, and asserts it by leaving the element
#   out, which `composition/converted.sqlc` reads as a factor of one. That two editions of one word
#   are one unit is a judgement this repository never makes in SQL, and an omission would let it
#   arrive as a blank instead.
#
# How the crossings are filed
#   Two file 1, 1 and 1 with a `boundOrigin` and a provenance, so the assertion has an author and a
#   note saying what would make it false; the third, in a fixture, files `unmeasured`, which is a
#   composer saying they looked at a real crossing and could not size it.
#   `checks/unit_crossing_without_a_factor` keeps it that way, and it is why no crossing goes
#   without a factor, and not by luck.
#
# An omission means nothing to convert
#   The parts that still leave the element out are all right to: their unit and their composed
#   layer's already agree, so a factor of one follows from what the document states, and there is
#   no author to name. That is the whole distinction the rule enforces: an omission may only ever
#   mean "nothing to convert".
#
# A factor typed blank is not an edge either
#   For a different reason: `unmeasured` says nobody sized the conversion, so there is no number to
#   put on the arrow.
#
# The `quantity = 'nameplate'` pin is a slice
#   It is declared here because it does not show in the key. A unit is a property of a claim, not of
#   a layer: `pm.claim.unit` is per position, and arithmetic/remainder.sqlc carries a
#   `not comparable` verdict for exactly the layer whose demand and nameplate disagree. So reading
#   the edge off one quantity takes one slice of the claims, and the total over all of them would be
#   a different relation. It is sound while no layer files two units across its quantities.
#
# That is a claim about the corpus, so a law holds it
#   algebra/layer_units.sqlc holds it over layers/quantities.sqlc, every time the laws run: each
#   layer has one unit across its quantities. The day a layer files two units, this graph and
#   checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close would be reading one quantity in five and saying nothing
#   about the rest, and the law is what says so.
#
# The word `nameplate` is checked
#   `quantity` is `public.layer_quantity`, so the pin below is a value of a declared type, and a
#   misspelling fails when this query is parsed. Spelled as free text, a misspelling would match
#   nothing, and a conversion graph with no edges is not obviously wrong to look at.
-- asrt:Part/asrt:factor at the nameplate, as part-layer-unit to composed-layer-unit.
SELECT DISTINCT
       p.part_unit     AS from_unit,
       p.composed_unit AS to_unit,
       p.factor_low, p.factor_mode, p.factor_high,
       p.composition AS filing, p.composed_layer AS layer
FROM (
    :compose(composition/part_quantities.sqlc)
) p
WHERE p.quantity = 'nameplate'
  AND p.factor_state = 'stated'
